  <h2>The Tunnel Service</h2>
  <p>
    The tunnel service lets you expose a server running on your own machine at a temporary public URL so you can demo work, test webhooks, and share development builds. It is a development tool. It is not a hosting platform, a content delivery network, an anonymizing proxy, or a production environment, and you agree not to use it as one.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tunnels are ephemeral. They exist only while your client is connected, and on the free plan they expire after a limited period, currently one hour. Current plan limits are published on <a href="https://slim.sh">slim.sh</a> and in the dashboard.</li>
    <li>Any URL you create is public to anyone who has it. Use the password protection option for anything you would not want a stranger to open, and do not expose production systems, customer data, or credentials through a Tunnel.</li>
    <li>We may display an interstitial warning page to first-time visitors of a Tunnel, identifying it as a temporary user-created development URL. You agree not to circumvent, hide, or instruct visitors to bypass this page for content served to the general public.</li>
    <li>You are solely responsible for everything served through your Tunnel, including all content, code, and data that reaches visitors through it.</li>
    <li>We may terminate any Tunnel, reclaim any subdomain, or disable any custom domain at any time, with or without notice, to protect the Service or its users.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Custom domains you connect to the Service must be domains you own or are authorized to use, and you must keep the required DNS records in place for them to continue working.
